#!/usr/bin/python3
"""The photo backup's rsync on dorkyrobot1: every path anchored at /Volumes/WD6TB.

Installed at ~/.local/bin/wd6tb-rsync on dorkyrobot1. dorkyrobot2's
~/Services/media-backup/backup.sh names it with --rsync-path.

Why it exists. Until 2026-10-28 the backup's key on dr1 carried the forced
command `rrsync /Volumes/WD6TB`, so whatever dr2 asked for, rsync only ever saw
paths inside that drive and refused `..`. Since then the tailnet ssh to dr1 is
Tailscale SSH, which never reads authorized_keys: the forced command is gone
and the client runs whatever it names. Felix, 11-05: "we should make sure
everything works with tailscale", so the backup stays on the tailnet and asks
for this wrapper itself. It rebuilds the command line rrsync expects in
SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND (sshd's format, args backslash-escaped) and hands over to
the same rrsync, so the WD6TB-only rules, option checks and refusals are
exactly the ones the forced key had.

What it is and is not. It keeps a mistake in backup.sh (a wrong path, an
absolute one, a `..`) from ever writing outside WD6TB. It is not a lock
against someone on dr2: Tailscale SSH gives any dr2 login a full shell on dr1,
whatever this file says. That is the tailnet ssh policy's job, not the
backup's.

Over the -lan or cloudflare- path the old key's forced command still applies,
and it runs rrsync directly; backup.sh must not name this wrapper there.

Failure is loud without the ssh exit status (which Tailscale SSH on macOS
loses, tailscale/tailscale#18256): rrsync refusing closes the stream before the
rsync protocol finishes, and the client rsync on dr2 exits non-zero by itself.
"""
import os
import re
import sys

ROOT = "/Volumes/WD6TB"
RRSYNC = os.path.expanduser("~/.local/bin/rrsync")

# rrsync splits SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND on unescaped whitespace and strips the
# backslashes (braceexpand), as rsync's own client escapes for a remote shell.
def esc(arg):
    return re.sub(r"([^A-Za-z0-9._,+:/=@%^-])", r"\\\1", arg)

if sys.argv[1:2] != ["--server"]:
    sys.exit("wd6tb-rsync: only rsync --server, as rsync's --rsync-path runs it")

os.environ["SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND"] = "rsync " + " ".join(esc(a) for a in sys.argv[1:])
os.execv("/usr/bin/python3", ["/usr/bin/python3", RRSYNC, ROOT])
